{"content":[{"signature":"","thinking":"\nThe user wants me to implement a `solve(case)` function for a grammar-guided node reduction task. Let me analyze the requirements:\n\n1. Reduce a parse tree by removing nodes\n2. Never remove: root, nodes with required symbols, nodes with retained children\n3. Process deeper nodes before shallower ones\n4. Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\n5. Use node ID as stable tie-break at equal depth\n6. Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order\n\nLet me think about the algorithm:\n\n1. Build the tree structure from nodes\n2. Process nodes in order: deeper nodes first, then by node ID for tie-breaking\n3. For each node (that is removable and not root):\n   - Check if removing it would still leave all required symbols represented\n   - Check that it has no retained children\n   - If safe to remove, remove it\n4. Return the retained node IDs (sorted) and the removal order\n\nWait, let me re-read: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\"\n\nSo a node can be removed if:\n- It's removable\n- It's not root\n- Its symbol is not in required_symbols (or... wait, \"a required symbol\" might mean a node whose symbol is in required_symbols)\n- It has no retained children (all its children have been removed)\n- After removal, all required symbols are still represented\n\nThe order of processing: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" - process in descending depth order, with node ID as tie-break.\n\nLet me think about this more carefully. We process deeper nodes first. For each node in that order:\n- If it's removable\n- And its symbol is not in required_symbols (or: required symbols are still covered after removal)\n- And it has no retained children currently\n- Then remove it\n\nWait, \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" - this is a constraint check. So we try to remove a node, check if required symbols are still covered, and if yes, keep the removal.\n\nLet me re-read: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\"\n\nI think \"a required symbol\" means a node whose symbol is in required_symbols. But then \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" is saying: even if a node's symbol is in required_symbols, you could potentially remove it if there are other nodes with that symbol. But wait, \"Never remove... a required symbol\" seems absolute.\n\nActually, I think \"a required symbol\" means the root or nodes in required_symbols. Let me reconsider.\n\nActually re-reading: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" The three conditions are:\n1. root (the root node)\n2. a required symbol (a node whose symbol is in required_symbols)\n3. a node with retained children\n\nAnd separately: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" - this is an additional constraint.\n\nSo combining:\n- Don't remove root\n- Don't remove a node if its symbol is in required_symbols (hard constraint)\n- Don't remove a node that has retained children\n- Don't remove a node if doing so would leave some required symbol unrepresented\n\nWait, if we never remove a node with a required symbol, then condition 4 (all required symbols remain) would only matter if there are multiple nodes with the same required symbol and we're removing one...\n\nActually, I think I'm overcomplicating this. Let me re-read.\n\n\"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\"\n\nI think \"a required symbol\" here means a node whose `symbol` is in `required_symbols`. This is a hard constraint.\n\n\"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" This is an additional check - even if a node is removable and doesn't have a required symbol itself, removing it might indirectly affect the tree. But wait, if we never remove nodes with required symbols, how could required symbols become unrepresented? \n\nOh wait - maybe children of removed nodes are also removed! If we remove a node, its children become orphaned. Do we remove the subtree? That would explain why required symbols could become unrepresented.\n\nActually, looking at Perses-style reduction: when you remove a node, its children might be removed too (subtree deletion). So if we remove a node, all its descendants are also removed. In that case, if a descendant has a required symbol, that required symbol would be lost.\n\nSo the algorithm:\n1. Order nodes by depth (descending) then by node ID (ascending for tie-break)\n2. For each node in that order:\n   - Skip if: not removable, or is root, or has a required symbol\n   - Skip if: it currently has retained children\n   - Check: if we remove this node (and it has no retained children, so it's a leaf in the current retained tree), do all required symbols remain?\n   - If yes, remove it (add to removal order, mark as removed)\n\nWait, but if we process deeper nodes first and a node has no retained children, removing it means removing just that node (since it has no children in the retained tree). So the check is: does removing this single node leave all required symbols still present?\n\nSince we're processing deeper nodes first, by the time we consider a node, its children might already have been removed. The \"no retained children\" constraint means we can only remove nodes that are currently leaves in the retained subtree.\n\nLet me trace through case 0:\n\nNodes:\n- root (depth 0, removable=false, symbol=root)\n- n1 (depth 1, removable=false, symbol=s1, parent=root)\n- n2 (depth 1, removable=true, symbol=s2, parent=root)\n- n3 (depth 1, removable=false, symbol=s0, parent=root)\n- n4 (depth 2, removable=true, symbol=s1, parent=n2)\n- n5 (depth 2, removable=false, symbol=s2, parent=n3)\n- n6 (depth 2, removable=true, symbol=s0, parent=n1)\n\nrequired_symbols = [\"root\", \"s1\"]\n\nProcessing order (depth desc, then node ID asc):\n- depth 2: n4, n5, n6 (sort by ID: n4, n5, n6)\n- depth 1: n1, n2, n3 (sort by ID: n1, n2, n3)\n- depth 0: root\n\nn4: removable=true, symbol=s1 (required!), skip\nn5: removable=false, skip\nn6: removable=true, symbol=s0 (not required), no retained children, \n    check: if we remove n6, remaining symbols: root (root), s1 (n1), s2 (n2), s0 (n3), s1 (n4), s2 (n5)\n    required: root ✓ (root node), s1 ✓ (n1, n4)\n    → remove n6, add to removal_order\n\nNow n1 has no children (n6 removed).\n\nn1: removable=false, skip\nn2: removable=true, symbol=s2, has child n4 (retained), skip (has retained children)\nn3: removable=false, skip\nroot: removable=false, skip\n\nFinal retained: root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5\nRemoved: n6\n\nHmm, n2 can't be removed because n4 is still there. n4 can't be removed because it has required symbol s1.\n\nLet me verify: retained IDs sorted = [\"n1\", \"n2\", \"n3\", \"n4\", \"n5\", \"root\"]\nRemoval order = [\"n6\"]\n\nNow let me implement this:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    payload = case[\"payload\"]\n    nodes = payload[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(payload[\"required_symbols\"])\n    \n    # Build node map\n    node_map = {n[\"id\"]: n for n in nodes}\n    \n    # Build children map\n    children = {n[\"id\"]: [] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n[\"parent\"] is not None:\n            children[n[\"parent\"]].append(n[\"id\"])\n    \n    # Track retained nodes\n    retained = set(n[\"id\"] for n in nodes)\n    removal_order = []\n    \n    # Sort nodes: deeper first, then by node ID (ascending)\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\"depth\"], n[\"id\"]))\n    \n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node[\"id\"]\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue\n        if not node[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        if node[\"symbol\"] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        # Check if it has retained children\n        if any(c in retained for c in children[nid]):\n            continue\n        # Check if all required symbols remain after removal\n        # Since this node is a leaf (no retained children), removing it just removes this node\n        remaining_symbols = set(node_map[n][\"symbol\"] for n in retained if n != nid)\n        if required_symbols.issubset(remaining_symbols):\n            retained.remove(nid)\n            removal_order.append(nid)\n    \n    return {\n        \"retained_ids\": sorted(retained),\n        \"removal_order\": removal_order\n    }\n```\n\nWait, let me double-check the output format. The problem says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" I'll return a dict with those two keys.\n\nLet me trace through case 1 to verify:\n\nNodes:\n- root (depth 0, removable=false, symbol=root)\n- n1 (depth 1, removable=false, symbol=s1, parent=root)\n- n2 (depth 1, removable=true, symbol=s2, parent=root)\n- n3 (depth 1, removable=false, symbol=s0, parent=root)\n- n4 (depth 2, removable=true, symbol=s1, parent=n2)\n- n5 (depth 2, removable=false, symbol=s2, parent=n3)\n- n6 (depth 2, removable=true, symbol=s0, parent=n1)\n\nrequired_symbols = [\"root\", \"s1\"]\n\nSame structure! Same result: remove n6, then try n2 but it has n4 (retained), n4 has required symbol s1.\n\nCase 2: Same structure, same result.\n\nThe algorithm seems consistent. Let me clean up the implementation:\n\nThe response needs to be valid JSON with key \"implementation\" containing the Python source as a string. Let me write the final implementation.\n\nActually wait - I need to verify my understanding of the symbol check. The constraint \"Never remove... a required symbol\" - does this mean:\n(a) Never remove a node whose symbol IS in required_symbols, OR\n(b) Never remove a node if doing so would make a required symbol unrepresented\n\nI've been treating it as (a), and (b) is a separate condition (\"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\").\n\nThis interpretation seems consistent. Let me finalize.\n\nOne thing to double-check: \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" - I'm sorting by node ID as string. The IDs are \"n1\", \"n2\", etc., which sort lexicographically. \"n4\" \u003c \"n5\" \u003c \"n6\" in string comparison. This seems right.\n\nActually wait - n10 would come before n2 lexicographically. The IDs in these cases are all single-digit so it's fine, but the general solution should handle this. Since we're given these specific IDs as strings, lexicographic sorting on the ID strings is what's specified (\"node ID as stable tie break\").\n\nThe implementation looks good. Let me format it as a JSON response.\n","type":"thinking"},{"text":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    payload = case[\\\"payload\\\"]\\n    nodes = payload[\\\"nodes\\\"]\\n    required_symbols = set(payload[\\\"required_symbols\\\"])\\n\\n    # Validate required fields\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        for field in (\\\"id\\\", \\\"parent\\\", \\\"symbol\\\", \\\"depth\\\", \\\"removable\\\", \\\"utility\\\"):\\n            if field not in node:\\n                raise ValueError(f\\\"Node missing required field: {field}\\\")\\n    if \\\"required_symbols\\\" not in payload:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"payload missing required_symbols\\\")\\n\\n    node_map = {n[\\\"id\\\"]: n for n in nodes}\\n\\n    # Build children map\\n    children = {n[\\\"id\\\"]: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n[\\\"parent\\\"] is not None:\\n            children[n[\\\"parent\\\"]].append(n[\\\"id\\\"])\\n\\n    # All nodes start as retained\\n    retained = set(n[\\\"id\\\"] for n in nodes)\\n    removal_order = []\\n\\n    # Process deeper nodes first; use node ID (string) as stable tie-break at equal depth\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\\\"depth\\\"], n[\\\"id\\\"]))\\n\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node[\\\"id\\\"]\\n\\n        # Must still be retained\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Hard constraints: must be removable, not root, not a required symbol\\n        if not node[\\\"removable\\\"]:\\n            continue\\n        if node[\\\"symbol\\\"] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Must have no retained children (leaf in current retained subtree)\\n        if any(c in retained for c in children[nid]):\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Tentative removal: all required symbols must remain represented\\n        remaining_symbols = {node_map[n][\\\"symbol\\\"] for n in retained if n != nid}\\n        if not required_symbols.issubset(remaining_symbols):\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Commit removal\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        removal_order.append(nid)\\n\\n    return {\\n        \\\"retained_ids\\\": sorted(retained),\\n        \\\"removal_order\\\": removal_order\\n    }\\n\"}","type":"text"}],"id":"msg_01XIFH5S7IY6UrMyOKEBVSbP","model":"claude-opus-4-7","role":"assistant","stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2037},"cache_creation_input_tokens":2037,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"inference_geo":"not_available","input_tokens":80,"output_tokens":3383,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":2815},"service_tier":"standard"}}